Bound the catch-up after a stalled stream (Y2Linux).

The rate synchronization counts frames from one start time and, when it is
behind, sends without a pause until it has caught up. Whatever stops the
transfer for a while (a radio link that stalls, a write that blocks, a client
that delivers late) is followed by all the missed audio at once, as fast as
the link takes it. Headphones answer with a dropout and then fast playback
(Y2Reborn #87, Sony WH-1000XM4), and the player never sees it: nothing
underruns, the back-pressure just reaches the client.

Measured on a Y2 with AirPods Pro and the player moved away until the sound
broke up (btmon, 2026-11-10): 49 gaps over 60 ms in 5 minutes, the longest
2.88 s without a packet, then 6.1 s of audio in the next 3 s; 63 packets in
one 500 ms window where real time is 21. Nothing was dropped: 300.3 s of
audio went out in 299.8 s.

Now at most 200 ms of audio is owed after a stall (the start time moves
forward by the rest; the music continues from where it stopped, nothing is
skipped), and what is owed goes out at no more than 4/3 of real time, so the
headphones' buffer fills up again without a burst. A stream that is on time
or late by ordinary scheduling jitter is paced exactly as before. The rule
is in src/shared/y2-catchup.h (pure arithmetic, tested on the host by
tests/test_bluetooth_catchup.py); it applies to bluealsad's encoder threads
and to the ALSA plug-in's transfer thread, which share this code.

